WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.600
Good morning, John, we are about to have gone
around the sun again.

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:06.000
That's right, my next Vlogbrothers video will
be made in the year 2015.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.049
Over on SciShow, Michael Aranda just gave
us some of the science superlatives of 2014:

00:00:10.049 --> 00:00:15.259
the deepest diving whale ever recorded, the
most Earth-like planet, the highest CO2 emissions,

00:00:15.259 --> 00:00:17.400
and the warmest months ever recorded.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.500
2014 has been a big year in science, but it's
also been a big year in general, so here on

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:26.390
Vlogbrothers where things are more general,
let's talk about some of the biggest, bestest,

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:28.019
superlative-est things that happened this
year.

00:00:28.019 --> 00:00:31.580
First, with the very likely possibility that
this will have been the warmest year ever

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:35.820
recorded, and while we are an adaptable species,
and I do have a lot of faith in us, figuring

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:39.350
out how to grow all the food we need to grow
with water landing in different places than

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:43.410
it used to land, and protecting all of the
infrastructure we already have with new infrastructure

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.190
that's going to be very expensive... (groan).

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:47.230
But there's also good news.

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:51.020
2014 saw the youngest ever recipient of the
Nobel Peace Prize.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:54.870
Malala Yousafzai's acceptance speech made
me cry, it probably will make you cry too

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:58.400
if you watch it, and it also gave me hope
about some very depressing, seemingly hopeless

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.469
situations, so that's -- and she's 17.

00:01:01.469 --> 00:01:03.460
What was I doing when I was 17?

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:04.949
I can't even talk about it.

00:01:04.949 --> 00:01:05.949
I -- no.

00:01:05.949 --> 00:01:07.909
It was also probably the best year ever to
be a nerd.

00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:11.270
Sherlock came back, Orphan Black is awesome,
we're getting new Star Wars movies and new

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:15.781
Star Trek movies, and new Avengers movies,
we had a new X-Men movie, new Doctor, new

00:01:15.781 --> 00:01:17.070
Veronica Mars, new Cosmos.

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:21.660
We landed a probe on a comet, Curiosity realized
that it's definitely for sure roving around

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:25.509
on an ancient Martian lakebed, got a female
Thor, and a black Captain America.

00:01:25.509 --> 00:01:26.509
It's been a good year.

00:01:26.509 --> 00:01:30.720
I'll also venture to say that it's the first
year in decades when America broadly decided

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:35.970
that it had to confront its, you know, sort
of mass, like, racism.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:37.100
That's what it -- that's -- yeah.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:41.880
Michael Brown, Eric Garner, John Crawford,
12-year-old Tamir Rice, were all killed by

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.899
police officers and were all unarmed.

00:01:43.899 --> 00:01:48.520
In and of itself, that's actually not unusual,
which is a sad thing to have to say.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.619
Unarmed black men are shot and killed by police
officers every year.

00:01:51.619 --> 00:01:55.180
This isn't like a new explosion of police
shootings.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:58.840
What's different is all of the Americans,
mostly Americans of color, who stood up and

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:03.900
said that they have had enough, and advanced
a national dialogue about police wearing body

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:07.590
cameras, about the difficulties of being a
person of color in America, about the systemic

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:09.100
racism in the justice system.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:12.800
These are problems that we've always had,
but that we haven't talked about for a long

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.850
time, like somehow racism went away after
segregation.

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:20.270
Discussions about systemic sexism were also
advanced, with Emma Watson's he-for-she speech

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:23.970
at the United Nations, and that video that
got like 30 million views of this woman just

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:25.510
walking around New York being harassed.

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:29.490
One final first for you: here in Missoula,
Montana we had our first same-sex marriage

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:33.760
at the courthouse, which just, I can't believe
it took so long, but it happened.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:37.660
As with any year, there's been good and there's
been bad, and we talk more about the bad than

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:43.771
about the good, but maybe that will lead us
to fighting against the bad so that next year,

00:02:43.771 --> 00:02:47.770
in 2015, there will be less of it, though
I'm sure the amount of it that we have is

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:49.240
what we will mostly talk about.

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:50.240
Again.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:51.240
As always.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:54.260
With help from some people on Twitter, I've
created a playlist of some of the most important

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:59.520
YouTube videos of 2014, including Emma Watson
and Malala's speeches, and CGP Grey talking

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:02.780
about why maybe in the future, jobs won't
exist.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:04.240
Should you be happy or sad?

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:05.240
I'm not sure.

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:08.690
Everyone, thank you for another great year
here at Vlogbrothers, and John, I'll see you

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:09.310
on Tuesday.

